																		(Seeking Greenlight) Joke SCP - mind-influencing game code
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/pumgumgum" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6463807); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6463807&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1610115134" alt="Pumgumgum" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6463807)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/pumgumgum" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6463807); return false;" >Pumgumgum</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609261243 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Dec 2020 17:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4856751">
									

<p>A seemingly insignificant script which mysteriously appears in popular online multiplayers, generates a user account of which in game exposure can influence the mind.</p>
<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>SCP-XXXX is a script</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX embeds itself in online multiplayers, becoming uneditable</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX generates a fictitious user account: SCP-XXXX-1, always with the same username</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX-1 is mostly in-active, but will randomly enter short “active” periods.</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX-1 seems to innately know each player's interests.</li>
<li>Players who interact with SCP-XXXX-1 will develop an exponential desire to talk with it, claiming “he’s fun” and “he understands me”.</li>
<li>Players will start prioritising surveying SCP-XXXX-1 for it’s next “active” state over important self needs. Which will result in, if uncatered to, heavy physical and psychological damage</li>
<li>Players will exhibit stress and violence when deprived of contact with SCP-XXXX-1.</li>
<li>SCP-XXXX-1 seems to require this attention. The more players SCP-XXXX-1 surrounds itself with, the “happier” it feels and the more space SCP-XXXX takes. If SCP-XXXX-1 is deprived of players, it feels “sad” and SCP-XXXX will progressively diminish, before eventually switching game.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Central Narrative</strong>: Firstly, will be outlined both monitoring and containment instructions for SCP-XXXX. Followed by, procedures for SCP-XXXX-1 interviews and how players coming into &quot;contact&quot; with SCP-XXXX-1 are to be dealt with.</p>
<p>Secondly, a description will briefly detail SCP-XXXX and SCP-XXXX-1’s nature and odd properties. Focusing more heavily on SCP-XXXX-1 and how its it seduces players into frequenting it. SCP-XXXX-1 seems to be unaware of the damage it causes to players, of its origin and of how it innately know each player's interests.</p>
<p>Finally, an addendum will feature:</p>
<ul>
<li>A report about SCP-XXXX’s classification as an SCP. SCP-XXXX-1 was known long before, but it’s anomalous properties were only noticed recently, after <em>Dr. A</em> found a correlation between multiple events involving SCP-XXXX-1. In addition to SCP-XXXX’s containment.</li>
<li>A report by <em>Dr. B</em> explaining <em>Dr. A</em> is to be relegated of his duties as a foundation researcher. Explaining <em>Dr. A</em> mental state is no longer professional and his reports have become increasingly bias towards SCP-XXXX-1.</li>
<li>An interview between <em>Dr. B</em> and SCP-XXXX-1, in which they discuss it’s: origin, feelings, and relationship with players. During which SCP-XXXX-1 will express nostalgia about games and players and a wish to see <em>Dr. A</em> again.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber</strong>: This article seeks to create a fictitious origin for extreme IAD (Internet addiction disorder). This article writes about an inside joke the ChilloutVR community has created, in which an SCP is responsible for users spending their lives in virtual reality (and by extension any other online multiplayers).</p>
</div>
