			<div class="content" id="post-content-4857213">
									

<p>I'm not really a fan of an idea that boils actual addictions down to 'oh it's an anomaly', particularly ones that can be as quietly harmful to people as video game addictions can be.</p>
<p>Beyond this, the narrative feels&#8230;unfocussed? It kinda flits around various ideas: the behavioural patterns of the SCP, the way it affects players, the list of games it was part of; and it feels like these ideas never really come together to form a single, cohesive whole. It also feels like this could really do with a like&#8230;single affected player as the focus character, like, someone we can see slipping into worse and worse patterns of behaviour and whose plight is empathetic in a way that fills us with dread as we see them descend towards some final outcome that's terrible; but my concern is that the effect of the SCP is too compulsive for this to work well. That is to say: the SCP is effectively forcing them to become addicted, so there's no real like, choice or ability for them to like resist this or anything; whereas if the SCP was more&#8230;'seductive', then you can really like, have them be conflicted about whether they should stop and really give the narrative the conflict it needs that way.</p>
<p>I don't think it necessarily needs to be focussed in this specific manner; but it does feel like it all needs to come together in a way that makes it feel like this is telling a single, coherent story and it doesn't quite yet do that, for me.</p>

							</div>

								
					
